{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/885567-houserules-to-balance-a-crossover-setting","id":885567,"title":"Houserules to Balance a Crossover Setting","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-05-08T12:10:56-07:00","id":885567,"title":"Houserules to Balance a Crossover Setting","content":"So I'm helping build a crossover setting for a 2.0 game online. The game incorporates Vampire and Mage, Werewolf and will incorporate Changeling when it comes out. It has the potential for growth as we continue, and leaves open the option for demon at a l. I've been really excited by the new setting and been looking forward to implementing it. Thing is, as I look through mage I can't help but feel like there is almost an unfair advantage to anyone playing mage versus what I've seen from the other venues. Now mind you - I fully admit that I've not read every single power in the mage book and may have missed limitations. <br /> <br /> At the moment, a lot of my issues come from the withstand trait and the ability for a master with unmaking to automatically kill something it interacts with. Besides the fact that I don't particularly find myself fond of auto-kill effects (I think they are kind of cheap and hinder imagination), the fact that the Withstand trait can be overcome relatively easily by achieving an exceptional success (something that is made further easy by praxis or self-made rotes), makes me uncomfortable with this process in game. It makes me more uncomfortable when you have effects like acceleration (which I could see being recreated with Life or Forces) which allow you all the things celerity would without the cost (move at any point, interrupt, ecetera).<br /> <br /> Now: Mage is has been my favorite venue, with changeling coming up second. I understand that Mage is probably <i>meant</i> to be superior to the other venues mechanically speaking. But I would like to try to find a way to make it where there is some level of balance between such venues so that when dealing with crossover it isn't always the mage who ends up coming on top in the end - at least not when dealing with individuals of similar power levels.<br /> <br /> What I'm looking for is either rules which would empower another venue (Vampire or Werewolf, Potentially Demon - I haven't read Demon myself to know if it even needs it) to be on a level of danger similar or at least close to what a Mage appears to be, a case in point that I am wrong in my analysis and misjudging the levels of power, or rules limiting a mage (which is the least desired option, but if it is the simplest it may be the option). <br /> <br /> So far some thoughts that I've considered are of course limitations on withstand - I saw one user post what appears to be good house rules on them. I've also considered removing the option of automatically killing another target if it is supernatural. Both of these thoughts are simple and easy, but I'd like to look at other options before suggesting them to my group. <br /> <br /> Another thought that I had (mostly due to reading the similarities between celerity and acceleration) were not to allow mages the ability to clash with supernaturals whose powers mimicked them - a vampire with celerity, who spent vitae to get their effect, will get their effect rather than incite a clash. This seems harsh but also forces a mage to get creative when dealing with a creature who uses similar tactics to them, forcing them to 'switch things up' when dealing with that kind of entity. <br /> <br /> It's a bit long winded, but I'm hoping it gets across my query. If anyone has any suggestions that might be applicable towards the goal of balance or near-balance, it would be greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> P.S. ... Time Magic is terrifying. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T12:36:14-07:00","id":885591,"title":"","content":"What I've done so far with my game that I'll be running for a vampire, werewolf and a demon (just started) is the following.<br /> <br /> Each character gets 2 supporting cast as per the wolf blooded created in the building the pack section with an appropriate power (3~XP worth) substituted in for the tell. [This was done so the werewolf could have a pack out of the gate without using all of their experience on retainers.]<br /> <br /> The next was each would have a source of power supplied for free. Vampires would be able to feed from their supporting cast for 2 or 4 vitae depending on their build (producer merit). Werewolves would have a locus of rank 1 ir 2 with their totem taking a share of the produced essance so they'd have 2 to 4 essance a day (rank of locus in response to vampire's supporting cast). [ The main reason for this was so each character would have equal amounts of their fuel without needing to go looking for every single point and 'wasting' game time.]<br /> <br /> So my suggestions are less house rules on changing the rules but having the players on equal footing. With a mage in the group there will need to be a gentleman's agreement that they won't overshadow other players in their respective fields. The only hacks that could get around this would be to take away much of what makes a mage a mage (extreme flexibility mostly) so I personally feel it'd be best to just make sure players know each others concepts and work together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T12:47:58-07:00","id":885604,"title":"","content":"What if you consider Unmaking effects targeting living patterns aimed spells always? This way they get to contend with defense on top of the easily bypassed withstand mechanic.<br /> <br /> You could ditch the negation of withstand entirely, but Rotes and Praxis still are a problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mr.F.I.X.</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/885567-houserules-to-balance-a-crossover-setting?p=885591#post885591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I've done so far with my game that I'll be running for a vampire, werewolf and a demon (just started) is the following.<br /> <br /> Each character gets 2 supporting cast as per the wolf blooded created in the building the pack section with an appropriate power (3~XP worth) substituted in for the tell. [This was done so the werewolf could have a pack out of the gate without using all of their experience on retainers.]<br /> <br /> The next was each would have a source of power supplied for free. Vampires would be able to feed from their supporting cast for 2 or 4 vitae depending on their build (producer merit). Werewolves would have a locus of rank 1 ir 2 with their totem taking a share of the produced essance so they'd have 2 to 4 essance a day (rank of locus in response to vampire's supporting cast). [ The main reason for this was so each character would have equal amounts of their fuel without needing to go looking for every single point and 'wasting' game time.]<br /> <br /> So my suggestions are less house rules on changing the rules but having the players on equal footing. With a mage in the group there will need to be a gentleman's agreement that they won't overshadow other players in their respective fields. The only hacks that could get around this would be to take away much of what makes a mage a mage (extreme flexibility mostly) so I personally feel it'd be best to just make sure players know each others concepts and work together.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't think this translates well to an online environment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T13:06:56-07:00","id":885620,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/885567-houserules-to-balance-a-crossover-setting?p=885604#post885604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Don't think this translates well to an online environment.</div> </div> </div> </div>If they are going to be working together instead of each going their separate ways I think it should work. The main purpose of the supporting cast in my opinion is to enable them to still pursue or at least maintain personal ventures/goals while working on joint ventures. However, if they all simply exist within the same world but do not interact with each other I admit what I've presented probably won't fit the bill. <br /> <br /> Or is there a separate reason why you feel this wouldn't work? If so please share. I admit it was just a quick way to make my group happy and enable them to play their characters the way they wanted (werwolf was happy as long as they had a pack and was ok with wolf blooded).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T13:18:25-07:00","id":885634,"title":"","content":"I think OP refers to a massive online games such as Mushes, it assumes potential PVP, which is why it's an issue, if it's not massive, then the players get rounded up like in TT, where murdering your fellow PCs is more bad form than if you enter a random scene and blast the vampire for being extra creepy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T13:37:02-07:00","id":885648,"title":"","content":"Okay, this might not be the advice you want, but hear me out.<br /> <br /> There is no way to balance Vampires, Werewolves and Mages in the conventional, D&amp;D sense. The games are so very different and the themes so disparate that reconciling them is all but impossible. And that's fine.<br /> <br /> What you <b>can </b>do is create a balanced experience for your players, to this end I recommend the following:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Moderate Character's Focus</b>. If two players want to be investigators, one a Mage and the other a Vampire, in all likelihood the Mage will blow the vampire out of the god-damned water. Which might seem terrible and unbalanced, but frankly the problem would occur even with a Vampire and a Werewolf, or two Vampires. The issue is spotlight sharing, and two characters competing for the same moment to shine. This is where you come in. As the storyteller you should moderate character creation, and make sure that none of the characters obviously step on one another's toes. Don't be afraid to veto a concept. Sure, you might get some blowback, but most players would rather have a character who belongs in the story than waddle about on the sidelines or be spited by their fellows.</li> <li><b>Control The Spotlight. </b>Coming off the first point, what most players want isn't to have a perfectly balanced mechanical experience, but rather to have their time in the spotlight, to play up their aptitudes and character. I've run games with vastly disparate 'power levels' (100+ exp vampire and a starting Prommie, for instance) and experienced no troubles because I made certain all the characters had moments catered to them. If you struggle with this, consider that the old D&amp;D adage &quot;don't split the party&quot; -- then spit on it and send it right to hell. A group divided (or a lone character) can get into situations and conflicts that everyone together would clutter or bulldoze. That doesn't even mean you have to put them miles apart, even in a single scene if you create multiple focuses different characters can devote themselves to different elements.</li> <li><b>Convey Your Expectations. </b>Seriously, talk to your players, let them know what to expect of the game and that you are attempting to tell a story, not have a dungeon crawl. Let them know that this isn't a competition between splats, but a tale about people. You'd be shocked what hackneyed roleplaying stereotypes go out the door when players trust their GM and take interest in the affairs of other characters.</li> <li><b>Know Your Players</b>. The final, but probably most important point you: need to know what your players desire from the game, and where their out of character aptitudes lie. If the Vampire player has expressed their love of the conflict between man and beast, you'll probably want to cater to that aspect for them. Similarly, if you've got a player who loves the power of Creative Thaumaturgy, but isn't particularly creative themselves, you may need to hold their hand some in game such that they get the experience they desire.</li> </ol>Finally, a note on Mage. As potent as Mages are, and they are insanely potent, they don't have to compete. Creative Thaumaturgy can apply to others, and can grant the spotlight as much as it can steal it. For instance, a Mage could use time magic to fuck over an antagonist and steal the Werewolf's spotlight OR they could use it to accelerate the Werewolf, and take satisfaction as the Werewolf player has a joyous scene of hyperspeed battlemode. Indeed, that's probably the easier spell, and most satisfying narrative. Which, again, brings me back to character and expectation<br /> <br /> <b>TL;DR</b> - From my personal experience balance is something found in the players, not systems. I've seen full Supernatural splats get wholly outclassed by a dinky mortal, because that character's player was, not to put too fine a point on it, better at problem solving. Knowing these aptitudes, and working with them to craft an enjoyable story will do more for game balance than any number of mechanical hacks.<br /> <br /> <b>Edit: </b>I will offer one simple mechanics that helps crossover: Lashing Out. You know how Vampires can lash out with their Aura to Clash with certain disciplines? Yeah, expand that. Let splats Clash against each other using their respective Auras as a form of active defense. Then, at least, no one will ever feel like they got Unmade/Dominated/Cowered/Etc. without any defense. It doesn't solve every problem, but it solves the most irritating one: Getting oneshotted by magic and having no recourse whatsoever.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","user":"PenDragon","date":". 05-08-2016, 04:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T13:37:42-07:00","id":885650,"title":"","content":"Well for unmaking spells they are limited to Masters mages of at least gnosis 5 and out right killing someone cannot ignore the withstand raiting. So you would have to compare that to a similarly powered vampire or werewolf. <br /> For example vampires when frenzied add their blood potency to their resistance stats. Thats 5+ added to their withstand raiting.<br /> <br /> But to help with your dilemma here are somethings you can do.<br /> Allow vampire to use the blood beat system on page 276.<br /> Let your vampires uses the influence advantage from damnation city on page 233.<br /> This will allow you to have vampires grow as quickly as mages in power and show their great influence over the world.<br /> <br /> Let your werewolves use things from the werewolf the forsaken chronicle guide I, lI, &amp; III. Each of them give great options to change up werewolf give new powers and styles of play. Including the option to have your own spirit choir. Which by the way you may what to add the prerequisite of honorary spirit hank per dot in that merit.<br /> <br /> Demons are wicked powerful as is so maybe just let your player choice between the play style presented in the demon storyteller guide, starting at page 89. And if you really feel they need more let them take powers from the demon translation guide.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T17:08:36-07:00","id":885799,"title":"","content":"A lot of these look really nice, and I appreciate the feedback; Every bit is helpful IMO <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I think TBH in the online enviroment Pendragon hit the nail on the head as far as the most irritating issue (and I know that mage people aren't going to want to lose the unmaking effect, after all)<br /> <br /> I actually did pretty good at balancing the venues in 1.0. I figure I'll hit what I feel is an 'acceptable' level once I understand the game a bit better. I'll go ahead and test the aimed spell idea that Ephsy was referring to and perhaps also test the Lashing out mechanic for the venues.<br /> <br /> The scenario is for a larger online community, so sometimes its more difficult than table top (especially when you are with people you don't know well), and PVP is a lot of the major issue in that - so I do appreciate the ideas. Thing Pendragon hit the nail on the head with the most irritating issue (which I'd rather not feel like an issue <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)<br /> <br /> Thanks guys <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}